course: dictionary
lesson: 10174
title: Tidy: more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. «You help tidy up any mess you made before you go home.» Here tidy is a verb: it means to make a place neat by putting things away.
explain: Tidy can also be a describing word. A tidy room is neat and in order, with everything in its right place. Tidy handwriting is easy to read because the letters are neat and even.
explain: In everyday talk, tidy can also mean quite a lot. A tidy amount of money is a good sum, not a neat pile. Look at the words around the word to see which meaning fits.
fact: Tidy can be an adjective (tidy, tidier, tidiest) or a verb (tidy, tidies, tidied, tidying).
fact: As an adjective, tidy means neat and in order, with things in their right places.
fact: To tidy up means to make a place neat by putting things away.
fact: In informal talk, «a tidy amount» means quite a lot.
fact: A tidy room is safer, because toys and cables on the floor can make people trip.
exercise: «You help tidy up any mess you made before you go home.» What does tidy up mean here? | To make a mess on purpose | To paint the walls | *To make a place neat by putting things away | To sit down and have a rest
exercise: «A tidy room is safer: toys and cables on the floor can make people trip.» What does tidy mean in this sentence? | Quite a lot | Covered in paint | *Neat and in order, with things in their right places | Very old
exercise: Ruby says «a tidy amount of money» means neat piles of coins. What does tidy mean here? | Empty | Very old | *Quite a lot | Neatly stacked
test: Which word means neat and in order, with things in their right places? | Messy | *Tidy | Heavy | Loud
test: Mia says her handwriting is tidy. What does she mean? | She wrote it very quickly | *Her letters are neat and easy to read | She wrote it in pencil | She wrote a very long story
exam: Which meaning of tidy fits best in «Tidy up time!»? | Time for a nap | *Time to put everything away | Time to count money | Time to go outside
